      "outputs": {
        "findings": [
          {
            "id": "F1",
            "severity": "major",
            "claim": "Phase 4 offline-verification scope claims six scenarios from \u00c2\u00a710 but \u00c2\u00a710 contains seven rows; the concurrent-edit-conflict scenario is absent.",
            "evidence": "ROADMAP Phase 4 Scope states: '(no connectivity, failed notification, invalid rrule, missing persistUntilSeconds default, backgrounded timers, multi-day offline catch-up)' \u00e2\u20ac\u201d six items. SPEC \u00c2\u00a710 table has a seventh row: 'Concurrent edit conflict (parent edits, child completes simultaneously)' with required behavior 'clientSequence comparison in StateAdapter (\u00c2\u00a75.1) resolves the conflict deterministically.' Phase 4 introduces the composed application, the only layer that can exercise simultaneous write paths through the adapter; verifying this at the composed-system level is Phase 4's responsibility. Omission means no explicit acceptance gate for this offline behavior at integration time."
          },
          {
            "id": "F2",
            "severity": "major",
            "claim": "Residual mojibake in Phase 1 scope corrupts a normative validation rule.",
            "evidence": "ROADMAP line 25: 'persistUntilSeconds \u00c3\u00a2\u00e2\u20ac\u00b0\u00c2\u00a5 `intervalSeconds`' \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the byte sequence \u00c3\u00a2\u00e2\u20ac\u00b0\u00c2\u00a5 is a cp1252-decoded rendering of UTF-8 U+2265 (\u00e2\u2030\u00a5). The upstream task chain included a mojibake-repair step (urn:fnsr:task:002) before the applier (urn:fnsr:task:003), yet the artifact survives in the applied file. The affected text is inside the normative validation-rule summary for Phase 1; a developer parsing this literally will encounter a malformed constraint."
          },
          {
            "id": "F3",
            "severity": "major",
            "claim": "\u00c2\u00a74.1 invalid-rrule error-attachment mechanism is internally contradictory in the SPEC; the ROADMAP carries the ambiguity forward without surfacing it as a deferred decision, making Phase 2 implementation indeterminate.",
            "evidence": "SPEC \u00c2\u00a74.1: 'If unparseable, return `[]` and attach `ftm:error: \"invalid-rrule\"` to each element of the output.' If the output is `[]` (empty array), there are no elements to attach the error to \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the instruction is vacuously unexecutable. SPEC \u00c2\u00a710 gives a different formulation: '`ScheduleComputer` returns `[]` with `ftm:error: \"invalid-rrule\"`', implying the error is on the array object itself, not its elements. The two sections are inconsistent. ROADMAP Phase 2 ScheduleComputer scope states only 'invalid-rrule error attachment' without flagging the contradition or asking for a resolution. Phase 2 implementation will require a concrete decision (error property on the return array, a separate error channel, a wrapper object) that is currently unmade and untracked."
          },
          {
            "id": "F4",
            "severity": "major",
            "claim": "CompletionProcessor is declared a pure function (\u00c2\u00a74.3) but \u00c2\u00a711 requires it to cross-reference the User store; the ROADMAP schedules both in Phase 2 without resolving the architectural contradiction.",
            "evidence": "SPEC \u00c2\u00a74.3 signature: 'Input: TaskInstance, completedBy: userId, completedByRole, completedAt: ISO-8601 string. Output: TaskInstance (new copy).' No User collection in the signature. SPEC \u00c2\u00a711 CompletionProcessor rules: 'completedBy must be a valid userId present in the User store'; 'If completedByRole = \"parent\", completedBy must resolve to a User with role = \"parent\".' These require a read from the User store, violating the pure-function contract. ROADMAP Phase 2 scope for CompletionProcessor includes 'validation rules from \u00c2\u00a711 (completedBy userId check, role-to-assignedTo cross-check)' without noting that the signature must change or that validation must be pre-hoisted to the application flow layer. No deferred-decision entry covers this. \u00c2\u00a711 preamble says 'invariants must be enforced at object construction time, before any entity is passed to a computation module' \u00e2\u20ac\u201d which could support pre-hoisting, but the ROADMAP does not commit to either interpretation."
          },
          {
            "id": "F5",
            "severity": "minor",
            "claim": "Phase 1 claims 'All entity-level validation rules from \u00c2\u00a711' without defining the scope boundary; four CompletionProcessor \u00c2\u00a711 rules appear in Phase 2 but are not listed in Phase 1's NOT-in-scope section.",
            "evidence": "ROADMAP Phase 1 Scope: 'All entity-level validation rules from \u00c2\u00a711: `TaskDefinition` title length, `assignedTo` role check, recurring schedule completeness, `intervalSeconds` minimum, `persistUntilSeconds` \u00e2\u2030\u00a5 `intervalSeconds`; `TaskInstance` `dueAt` format, `clientSequence` positive-integer requirement.' SPEC \u00c2\u00a711 additionally contains four CompletionProcessor rules (completedBy userId existence, completedByRole enum check, child-completedBy-must-match-assignedTo, parent-completedBy-must-resolve-to-parent-role). These are scheduled in Phase 2 (ROADMAP Phase 2: 'validation rules from \u00c2\u00a711 (completedBy userId check, role-to-assignedTo cross-check)'), but Phase 1's NOT-in-scope section does not exclude them. A developer reading only Phase 1 cannot determine that \u00c2\u00a711 coverage is split across two phases. The qualifier 'entity-level' is undefined in both the SPEC and the ROADMAP."
          },
          {
            "id": "F6",
            "severity": "minor",
            "claim": "Phase 3 LocalStorage adapter scope does not explicitly assert \u00c2\u00a75.1 conflict resolution, unlike the in-memory adapter which does.",
            "evidence": "ROADMAP Phase 3 Scope for in-memory adapter: 'last-writer-wins conflict resolution per \u00c2\u00a75.1 \u00e2\u20ac\u201d higher clientSequence wins; equal sequence \u00e2\u2020\u2019 later updatedAt wins; both equal \u00e2\u2020\u2019 incoming entity wins.' Phase 3 Scope for LocalStorage adapter: 'same StateAdapter interface; serializes JSON-LD to localStorage; silent fallback to in-memory if localStorage unavailable per \u00c2\u00a75.' The phrase 'same StateAdapter interface' implies behavioral parity, but \u00c2\u00a75.1 conflict resolution is a behavioral requirement, not an interface signature requirement. A developer implementing the LocalStorage adapter without reading the in-memory adapter's scope description could omit \u00c2\u00a75.1 compliance."
          },
          {
            "id": "F7",
            "severity": "advisory",
            "claim": "The TaskDefinition immutability invariant (\u00c2\u00a73.2) and the `ftm:version` increment mechanic are unaddressed across all five phases.",
            "evidence": "SPEC \u00c2\u00a73.2: 'A TaskDefinition is the parent-authored specification of work to be done. It is never mutated after creation; updates produce a new version.' The `ftm:version` field (value `1` in the canonical example) implies a versioning protocol for task edits. No phase in the ROADMAP covers: (a) enforcement of the immutability invariant at the StateAdapter level (e.g., blocking saveTaskDefinition from overwriting an existing @id), (b) the version-increment mechanic for updates, or (c) how a parent edits a task (creates a new TaskDefinition with incremented version, retiring the old one?). The conflict resolution in \u00c2\u00a75.1 uses clientSequence, not version \u00e2\u20ac\u201d the relationship between the two fields for TaskDefinition updates is not addressed."
          },
          {
            "id": "F8",
            "severity": "advisory",
            "claim": "Pre-Phase-1 decision gates lack owner, deadline, or escalation path; Phase 1 cannot begin until resolved, but resolution is not formalized as a prerequisite artifact.",
