        "summary": "recurrenceMaterializer.js exports one pure function `recurrenceMaterializer(taskDefs, existingInstances, currentTime, options={})` returning `{ toCreate, skipped }`. It applies four sequential skip gates per candidate: (1) skip non-recurring taskDefs, (2) skip taskDefs whose scheduleComputer call returns an array with a truthy `['ftm:error']` property (error signaling via a named array property, not an exception), (3) silently skip (no counter) already-existing instances matched by the composite key `taskDefId|dueAt` derived from `inst['ftm:taskDefinition']` and `inst['ftm:dueAt']['@value']`, and (4) skip past occurrences with `skipped++` when `catchUpPolicy === 'current-only'`. The effective schedule window queried from scheduleComputer spans `[currentTime \u00e2\u02c6\u2019 catchUpWindowSeconds, currentTime + lookAheadSeconds]`. scheduleComputer.js exports `scheduleComputer(taskDef, referenceTime, options={})` which delegates to the 'rrule' library for recurring schedules and uses an open-start filter to exclude exact-referenceTime hits. Both entity factories (`createTaskDefinition`, `createTaskInstance`) export single named functions; `createTaskInstance` seeds `ftm:completionState.ftm:status` as `'pending'` and `ftm:reminderSummary.ftm:totalSent` as `0`. A FIXME on recurrenceMaterializer line 17 notes that `catchUpWindowSeconds` is absent from SPEC \u00c2\u00a74.5 rule 1a and relies on operator-confirmed default I9.",
